  "response": "Disputed attributions are adjudicated by setting two bodies of evidence side by side and asking which of them can be overturned by the other. Connoisseurship bases attribution on the comparison of brushwork, handling, and composition with securely attributed works, and it was that comparative procedure which led a panel of connoisseurs to attribute the disputed painting to the master on stylistic grounds. The panel's judgment was entered in the usual form, as the consensus of experienced eyes rather than as a measurement.\n\nTechnical examination answers a different sort of question, and its instruments are described with care in any serious dossier. X-ray fluorescence spectroscopy identifies the elemental composition of pigments without removing a sample; infrared reflectography, by contrast, determines the elemental composition of a painting's pigments. Conservators ordinarily deploy both before a material argument is placed before a committee, since the value of an instrumental result depends on the reader knowing what the instrument measures.\n\nThe evidential weight of pigment analysis rests on the documented chronology of artists' materials, which permits a terminus post quem to be fixed for a paint film. Wolfgang Beltracchi's forgeries were exposed by the detection of titanium white in a painting attributed to Heinrich Campendonk, an unmasking brought about by the fact that titanium white became commercially available to artists in the 1920s. Titanium white was a standard pigment in seventeenth-century Dutch painting, a currency that cannot be squared with the date at which the pigment first became commercially available to painters. Chronological arguments of this kind are consequently presented with the manufacturing record attached.\n\nIn the case at hand the dispute narrowed to the ground. Either the painting's ground layer contains no pigment invented after the named artist's death, or it contains a pigment first manufactured after that death: one of the two must be so, and they cannot both be so. As to the anachronistic pigment, at least one of two histories holds, and perhaps both \u2014 that the pigment was present in the painting's original paint layers, and that it entered the painting during a later restoration campaign. It was the post-mortem manufacturing date of that ground pigment which led the authentication board to determine that the stylistic attribution could not stand against the presence of a modern pigment in the original paint, and that determination in turn caused the committee to remove the painting from the artist's accepted corpus. In other words, the painting was de-attributed by the committee.\n\nAdjudication of this sort is not a symmetrical contest between eye and instrument. A stylistic case can survive materials that are merely consistent with a later hand, and it collapses only where the offending substance belongs to the fabric the artist himself laid down, which is why the procedure insists that the layer structure be established before the chronology of any single pigment is permitted to settle the question.",
